speechtext:
The secretary treasurer goes from $4,620 to $5,070; the assistant secretary treasurer, from $3,480 to $3,690; the head clerk, from $1,525 to $2,520-apparently that is merely a provision; the position has not been filled-principal clerk, no increase; another principal clerk, from $2,220 to $2,340; still another, from $1,425 to $1,980; clerks, grade 4, $1,800 to $1,860, $1,800 to $1,860, $1,500 to $1,650, $1,380 to $1,410; stenographers, grade 3, $1,560 to $1,620, $1,500 to $1,560, SI200 to $1,410; typist, grade 2, no increase;

Supply-Trade-National Research
office appliance operator, grade 3, SI,260 to SI,320; clerk, grade 2, $990 to $1,155, $900 to $1,110; stenographers, grade 2, $1,200 to $1,260, $1,140 to $1,200, $1,012 to $1,110; office appliance operator, grade 2, $1,140 to $1,200.

It is usually a matter of
negotiation. I do not know that I would care to say that there is any absolutely definite basis which would apply to all the services;
I am sure there is not. Perhaps some of it is a matter of inheritance; they got such and such a subsidy last year. In some cases I have cut the subsidy down because I felt conditions justified this, while in other cases the representation may have been very strong to maintain the present subsidy; in some cases it has been reduced, and in some cases increased. The basis is just about the same as with freight rates. I remember very well one time asking the head of the freight rates department of a certain Canadian railway upon what they based their rates; was it according to service rendered? He said no, just what the traffic would bear.
